  [1mWhy the module holds a base URL and not the full login URL:[0m the host is a property of the environment, the [38;5;153m/login[39m path
  is a property of the login feature. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m composes [38;5;153m${settings.apiBaseUrl}/login[39m. That way adding a second endpoint
  later doesn't mean touching [38;5;153msettings.js[39m at all — which is the "easier to change environments" goal, generalized.

  [1mData flow.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls [38;5;153mresolveSettings(location.hostname)[39m once at module scope. Passing the hostname in as an
  argument (rather than reading it inside [38;5;153msettings.js[39m) is the whole reason the module stays testable without a browser.

  [1mError handling.[0m An unrecognized hostname throws an [38;5;153mError[39m naming the hostname and listing the known ones. Because the
  call sits at module scope, this fails at page load rather than at form submit — so a misconfigured host breaks visibly
  instead of quietly POSTing credentials to whichever backend happened to be the default. That's deliberate: it means a
  new hostname (a preview deploy, say) hard-fails until it's added to the table. I think that's the right trade for an
  auth endpoint, but say so if you'd rather it warn and fall back to local.

  [1mScope note.[0m [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is currently referenced only inside a comment — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub. I'll wire
  [38;5;153msettings.apiBaseUrl[39m into that same commented stub and update the comment, without turning the stub into a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m.
  Making the login call real is a separate request.

  [1mFiles touched:[0m [38;5;153msettings.js[39m (new), [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (remove the constant, add the import), [38;5;153mindex.html[39m (add [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m).

  One thing the design needs from you. This project has no linting, formatting, or test infrastructure at all, and
  that's cheapest to decide now — the pure [38;5;153mresolveSettings[39m function is the kind of thing a single test pins down
  permanently.
